The period of revolution (T) is related to the orbital radius (r) by Kepler's third law, which can be expressed as T^2 = (4π^2 / GM) * r^3, where G is the gravitational constant and M is the mass of the Earth. Since the orbital radius (r) increases when the speed decreases, the period of revolution (T) will also increase because T is directly proportional to the square root of r^3.

